# Firmware (ESP32 / WT32-ETH01)

- PlatformIO project root is `firmware/`. Always `cd firmware && pio run`.
  `src_dir` / `include_dir` resolve relative to `firmware/platformio.ini`.
- `firmware/src/main.cpp` was split into modules (it used to be one 5245-line
  file). Read only the module you need; the map:

  | File | Owns |
  |---|---|
  | `src/main.cpp` | `setup`/`loop`, sensors, display, serial/button, `setStatus`. Also **defines** every global. |
  | `src/cof_state.h` | Umbrella header. Include this (not the others). Types, `extern` globals, `LteMqttClient`. |
  | `src/cof_api.h` | Prototypes of all 175 functions, grouped by owner. No state. |
  | `src/net_paths.cpp` | Ethernet/WiFi/LTE selection, probes, DNS, holdoff. |
  | `src/modem_at.cpp` | AT transport, watchdog, radio recovery. |
  | `src/sms_voice.cpp` | SMS, calls, TTS/audio upload. |
  | `src/mqtt_io.cpp` | Connect/publish, deferred queue, telemetry JSON. |
  | `src/lte_pdp.cpp` | PDP activation (NETOPEN/CNACT), LTE peer. |
  | `src/ota_config.cpp` | Manifest, `performOta`, rollback guard, config apply. |
  | `src/at_parse.cpp` | Pure AT string/CEER parsers. |
  | `src/sensors_display.cpp` | I2C scan, SHT31, DS18B20, OLED. |

  Include order matters: modules include `cof_config.h` **then** `cof_state.h`.
- Timing constants live in `firmware/include/cof_config.h` (moved out of
  `main.cpp`). GPIO / APN / MQTT defaults are there too.
- A function's default arguments are declared **once**, in `cof_api.h`. Do not
  re-add them to a definition in a `.cpp`.
- Prefer a **new chat** for firmware work; don't mix it into hardware/KiCad threads.

## OTA release

The agent compiles and publishes `ota/`; the user only presses **Forzar chequeo OTA**.
Never ask for a USB reflash for a normal firmware drop.

Known process holes in this flow (VPS never auto-syncs, raw GitHub caching, no
"OTA rejected" signal) are listed in `docs/ops/BACKLOG.md` §P0. Read it before
touching `scripts/release-fw.*`.

1. Bump `COF_FIRMWARE_VERSION` in `firmware/include/cof_config.h` above the version
   currently in `ota/manifest.json`.
2. Publish (compiles, copies bin, updates sha256, commits, pushes):
   - Linux: `./scripts/release-fw.sh -y`
   - Windows: `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File scripts\release-fw.ps1 -y`
3. On the VPS **before** OTA: `cd /opt/callonfail && git pull`

> The VPS checkout is what serves `firmware.bin`; nothing syncs it automatically.
> Skipping the pull makes the device reject the update on sha256. See
> `docs/ops/BACKLOG.md` §P0.


Flask serves `https://app.callonfail.com.ar/ota/firmware.bin` from `ota/` (`OTA_DIR`).
The device reads the GitHub manifest, then downloads that VPS URL.

`scripts/push.sh` / `push.ps1` are git-only. Firmware releases use `release-fw`.

## OTA safety (remote devices)

The device is often only reachable over the network, so an OTA that bricks it
cannot be recovered by hand. Two guard rails exist in `performOta`:

- **sha256 check**: the image is hashed while streaming to flash and the slot is
  only committed if it matches `firmware.sha256` in the manifest. A manifest
  without a valid 64-char sha256 is refused outright.
- **Rollback**: `initOtaRollbackGuard` / `serviceOtaRollbackGuard` call
  `esp_ota_mark_app_valid_cancel_rollback()` only after the new image proves it
  works (MQTT connected + `kOtaConfirmMinUptimeMs` uptime). If it never gets
  healthy, the device reboots and the bootloader falls back to the old slot.
  This relies on `CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE=y` in the core sdkconfig.

**Pending**: `performOta` uses `client.setInsecure()`, so TLS is not verified.
Integrity is covered by the sha256, but the transport is not authenticated.
Fixing this means pinning the Let's Encrypt chain in the firmware.

## Network paths

Ethernet > WiFi > LTE, plus the rule that an interface is only "the internet" if
the broker is actually reachable through it (link + DHCP is not enough). Read
`docs/device/NETWORK_PATHS.md` before touching anything network related: it
documents the health flags, the probe rules, why `WiFi.hostByName()` must not be
called from `loop()`, and the publishing-liveness thresholds (in particular why
`kMqttSilenceReconnectMs` must stay above the telemetry interval).
